{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014e5CCsvEboq6q6zRfqb9mt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B8y7exboEJf8Dsx1R8b9TE","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Review Report: Psychometric Monitoring for LLM Benchmark Contamination\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nThe paper proposes a psychometric monitoring framework for LLM benchmark contamination grounded in Item Response Theory (IRT). Three contributions: (1) DIF analysis as an RL-robust contamination signal on MMLU (4,312 models × 11,847 items), achieving TPR=0.58 for large effects with power retention ratio 0.885 under RL training; (2) a dual-signal ensemble (DIF + ConStat) achieving full-spectrum TPR=0.68 with transparent DVD failure mode analysis; (3) within-subject bifactor IRT equating for contamination-aware item replacement, establishing 15% as the practical replacement bound.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 — Methods and Experiments\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Cross-disciplinary insight.** Reframing benchmark contamination as a DIF problem is a genuinely insightful contribution. DIF's theoretical RL-robustness argument — that it operates on population-level behavioral patterns (ICC) rather than within-model output distributions — is well-motivated and structurally distinct from existing detectors.\n\n**S2. Sound psychometric methodology.** The choice of ETS C classification over p-value-only testing, the 2PL IRT parameterization, and effect-size-based thresholds (δ ≥ 1.0 for C-level) all follow established psychometric best practices. The bifactor model specification with general + 57 subject-specific factors is appropriate for MMLU's heterogeneous domain structure.\n\n**S3. Honest reporting of negative results.** The DVD failure mode analysis is exemplary. Rather than cherry-picking the 2-signal result, the paper provides a transparent root-cause analysis of why generation variance fails on multiple-choice formats (Cohen's d = 0.38, insufficient separation). The principled 3→2 signal downgrade, supported by the ablation showing only +0.02 TPR gain from DVD, demonstrates intellectual honesty.\n\n**S4. Actionable operational bounds.** The graceful degradation analysis (Table 16) provides concrete deployment guidance: 15% replacement maintains τ ≥ 0.93 and α ≥ 0.84. The distinction between theoretical upper bound (25% under perfect calibration) and practical limit (15% given current generation quality) is a useful contribution for benchmark maintainers.\n\n**S5. Student simulation calibration.** The comparison of prompt-based targeting (ρ = 0.32) vs. student simulation (ρ = 0.63) is a valuable standalone result demonstrating that empirical calibration through model populations dramatically outperforms LLM self-assessment for difficulty estimation.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). RL-robustness claim critically underpowered.** The paper's headline contribution — DIF's RL-robustness — rests on only n = 35 RL models (23 documented + 12 inferred from pipeline descriptions). Of these, only 21 are \"heavy RL\" (≥200 steps). This is insufficient for the strength of the claim. Specific concerns:\n- No power analysis for the retention ratio comparison (0.885 vs. 0.689). With n = 35, the confidence interval around DIF's retention ratio likely overlaps with ConStat's (0.841).\n- No equivalence testing: a non-significant difference between DIF and ConStat retention is null-result reasoning, not evidence of equivalence.\n- \"RL intensity\" is measured as a binary proxy (< 200 vs. ≥ 200 steps), which is crude. The actual RL reward function, training data overlap, and PPO hyperparameters matter more than step count.\n- The comparison is not apples-to-apples: DIF operates at the group level (cohort-level TPR), while Min-K% and others operate at the model level (per-model AUROC). This metric asymmetry is never discussed.\n\n**W2 (Major). Ground truth circularity.** The primary detection metric (TPR = 0.58 for δ ≥ 1.4) is partially tautological: δ IS the DIF effect size statistic, so conditioning TPR on δ ≥ 1.4 essentially asks \"among items where DIF detects a large effect, how often does DIF detect it?\" The external validation via MMLU-CF yields only κ = 0.14 (slight agreement), which is too weak to serve as independent ground truth. The GSM8K/GSM1K validation (ρ = 0.22, p = 0.014) provides meaningful directional support but is not strong enough to anchor the detection accuracy claims.\n\n**W3 (Major). Contamination-capability confound unaddressed.** DIF detects differential item easiness across temporal cohorts. But post-2024 models are not only more contaminated — they are also more capable, especially in STEM domains. If a model family improves disproportionately in physics due to better training data (not contamination), DIF will flag physics items. The paper provides no analysis to disentangle this confound:\n- No within-family comparison (e.g., Llama-3 base → instruct → RL on the same items)\n- No domain-specificity analysis of DIF flags vs. known capability trajectories\n- Total-score conditioning in IRT is insufficient when improvement is domain-specific — the bifactor model's subject-specific factors could help here but are not used for this purpose\n\n**W4 (Moderate). Ensemble construction lacks rigor.** The weighted fusion with optimized weights (DIF: 0.58, ConStat: 0.42) is presented without cross-validation. The optimized fusion (TPR = 0.73 at δ ≥ 1.4) actually underperforms naive union (TPR = 0.79) at the cost of lower FPR (0.082 vs. 0.110). Whether this tradeoff is worthwhile is application-dependent but never discussed. FPR = 0.082 on 11,847 items implies ~970 false alarms — the operational cost of investigating and potentially replacing ~970 clean items is substantial and unaddressed.\n\n**W5 (Moderate). Equating quality gaps undermine replacement claims.** Generated items show systematic quality degradation relative to originals: discrimination −0.27 (0.93 vs. 1.20), factor loading −0.09 (0.38 vs. 0.47), distractor effectiveness −0.17 (0.61 vs. 0.78). These gaps are acknowledged but their implications are understated. With factor loadings 19% lower, the replacement items measure a somewhat different construct. Cross-subject equating (ρ = 0.87) with 23% unstable subjects is presented as \"future improvement\" but fundamentally limits the equating claim's scope.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: Can you provide bootstrap confidence intervals for the DIF retention ratio and test whether it significantly exceeds ConStat's?\n- Q2: What percentage of DIF-flagged items fall in STEM vs. humanities, and how does this compare to known capability improvement trajectories?\n- Q3: Have you considered using the bifactor model's subject-specific factors as covariates in the DIF analysis to control for domain-specific capability gains?\n\n### Minor Issues\n- Table numbering jumps (some referenced tables don't appear in the main text — likely in appendix)\n- The paper could benefit from a system diagram showing the full pipeline flow\n- Cumulative Bayesian DIF (Section 5.9) is interesting but the practical caveat (1-2 years of accumulation) should be more prominent\n\n**R1 Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nJustification: The conceptual contribution (DIF for contamination) is sound and the experimental coverage is comprehensive. However, the three major weaknesses — underpowered RL validation, ground truth circularity, and the contamination-capability confound — collectively undermine the paper's central claims. The paper makes ambitious claims that the evidence does not yet fully support.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 — Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Genuine cross-disciplinary bridge.** Applying DIF to LLM contamination is not a trivial mapping. The key insight — that contamination creates differential item functioning between exposed and unexposed model cohorts, analogous to test bias between demographic groups — requires substantive reconceptualization. The \"groups\" are temporal cohorts, \"ability\" is IRT-estimated model capability, and the \"bias\" is contamination-induced easiness. This reframing opens a mature 40-year psychometric literature to the LLM evaluation community.\n\n**S2. Theoretically grounded RL-robustness argument.** The argument that DIF resists RL smoothing because it operates on the ICC (population-level θ → P(correct) relationship) rather than output distributions has real theoretical depth. This is not an ad-hoc empirical observation but a structural property of the method.\n\n**S3. End-to-end system contribution.** No prior work connects detection (DIF) → diagnosis (ensemble with failure mode analysis) → remediation (IRT equating with calibrated replacement). While individual components have predecessors, the integrated pipeline is novel as a system.\n\n**S4. Calibrated narrative.** The paper's self-correction from \"self-healing benchmarks\" to \"psychometric monitoring framework with known operating bounds\" is refreshingly honest. Claims are generally well-calibrated to evidence (e.g., \"complementary signal\" rather than \"only survivor,\" within-subject equating \"pilot\" rather than full equating solution).\n\n**S5. GSM8K external validation.** Cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K with an independent ground truth (GSM1K) provides convergent evidence. The lower power on math reasoning benchmarks is honestly reported and attributed to pattern vs. item memorization differences.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). Related work critically incomplete.** The paper cites only 16 references — far below the norm for a top venue submission tackling a well-studied problem. Specific gaps:\n- The \"Psychometric methods in NLP evaluation\" paragraph mentions IRT applications but the citation coverage is thin. Works like Lalor et al. (2019), Rodriguez et al. (2021), and Vania et al. (2021) are in the bibliography but not adequately discussed.\n- Most critically, Tao et al. (ICLR 2026) — which directly addresses RL-robust contamination detection — appears to be a direct competitor that is not cited or compared against. If this work exists, omitting it is a serious oversight.\n- The dynamic benchmarks discussion dismisses LiveBench and DyVal in one sentence each without substantive comparison.\n- Missing broader context: no discussion of data documentation practices (Mitchell et al., 2019; Gebru et al., 2021), model evaluation frameworks (Liang et al., 2023 — HELM), or contamination in instruction tuning specifically.\n\n**W2 (Major). Partial exploitation blind spot.** The RL-robustness argument assumes contaminated models fully exploit memorized items (answering all of them correctly). In practice, contamination is partial — a model may have seen 60% of MMLU in training but only memorize a fraction. Partial exploitation shrinks the DIF effect size below the detection threshold, creating a systematic blind spot. The paper does not analyze detection power as a function of exploitation rate. This is theoretically distinct from R1's sample-size concern — it is about the fundamental signal strength, not the statistical power to detect it.\n\n**W3 (Moderate). Growing Pains differentiation inadequate.** Polo et al. (2026) uses multidimensional IRT for extensible evaluation — the paper dismisses it in one sentence as addressing \"score comparability rather than contamination detection or remediation.\" But the technical overlap is substantial: both fit IRT models to large response matrices, both address temporal comparability, and Growing Pains' extensible evaluation framework could potentially subsume the equating component of this work. A serious comparison is needed.\n\n**W4 (Moderate). Novelty framing overclaimed.** DIF has been applied to NLP evaluation before (Lalor et al. use IRT for evaluation, Rodriguez et al. discuss item properties). The step from \"IRT for LLM evaluation\" to \"DIF for LLM contamination\" is a natural extension within an established trend. The paper should frame its novelty as \"bringing mature, validated psychometric tools to an urgent new problem\" — emphasizing the problem-level contribution and the empirical validation — rather than implying methodological novelty. The methods are borrowed; the application is new.\n\n**W5 (Minor). Impact barriers not discussed.** Practical deployment requires: (a) access to the full response matrix (many models are API-only with no per-item results), (b) reliable temporal cohort labels, (c) sufficient model population size for IRT fitting. These barriers limit the framework's applicability beyond curated datasets like METABENCH. The paper should discuss who can actually use this framework.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: Are you aware of Tao et al. (ICLR 2026)? If so, how does your approach compare?\n- Q2: What is DIF's detection power as a function of partial exploitation rate (e.g., if a model memorizes an item but answers correctly only 70% of the time due to partial recall)?\n- Q3: Could Growing Pains' extensible evaluation framework be extended to include contamination detection, potentially subsuming your equating contribution?\n\n### Minor Issues\n- 16 references is below expectations; aim for 35-50\n- The abstract could mention the contamination-capability confound as a limitation\n- IFT contribution (Section 5.11) feels like padding — the +0.04 TPR gain is marginal\n\n**R2 Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nJustification: The cross-disciplinary contribution is genuine and the end-to-end system is valuable. The calibrated narrative and honest negative results (DVD failure, equating limitations) demonstrate scientific maturity. However, the incomplete related work and novelty overclaiming are concerning for a top venue. The paper sits at the borderline — the idea is strong enough to merit publication, but the execution and positioning need meaningful revision.\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Convergence\n\n### Points of Agreement\nBoth reviewers agree that:\n1. The DIF reframing of contamination is a genuine, non-trivial cross-disciplinary contribution\n2. The RL-robustness claim has theoretical merit but insufficient empirical evidence\n3. The DVD failure mode analysis and calibrated narrative are positive qualities\n4. The end-to-end pipeline has system-level value\n5. Individual component execution has significant gaps\n\n### Key Disagreement\nR1 (score 3) weights **methodological rigor** — finding the execution falls short of the claims. R2 (score 4) weights **conceptual contribution** — finding the idea and system design strong enough despite execution gaps. This is a classic novelty-vs-rigor tradeoff.\n\n### Top 3 Critical Issues (Converged)\n\n**Critical Issue 1: Contamination-Capability Confound (R1-W3).** The most underappreciated concern. Post-2024 models are both more contaminated AND more capable. DIF cannot distinguish domain-specific capability improvement from contamination-induced differential easiness without additional controls. Without within-family analysis (e.g., same architecture at different training stages), all DIF detection results are potentially confounded. The bifactor model's subject-specific factors could be leveraged for this but are not.\n\n**Critical Issue 2: RL-Robustness Insufficiently Supported (R1-W1 + R2-W2).** Two complementary concerns: (a) underpowered sample (n=35 RL models, crude binary intensity proxy, no equivalence testing), and (b) unanalyzed partial exploitation scenario where contamination benefit is incomplete. Together, these substantially weaken the paper's headline claim. The theoretical argument is compelling; the empirical support is not.\n\n**Critical Issue 3: Ground Truth Circularity and Validation Weakness (R1-W2).** The primary TPR metric is partially tautological (conditioning on the DIF statistic itself). External validation is weak: MMLU-CF κ=0.14, GSM1K ρ=0.22. The paper needs either stronger external ground truth or substantially reframed accuracy claims that acknowledge the circularity.\n\n### Additional Significant Issues\n- **Related work gaps** (R2-W1): 16 references insufficient; missing direct competitor Tao et al. (ICLR 2026)\n- **Ensemble rigor** (R1-W4): No cross-validation; FPR=0.082 → ~970 false alarms operationally\n- **Equating quality gaps** (R1-W5): Generated item quality systematically lower; cross-subject equating limited\n- **Novelty framing** (R2-W4): Should present as \"mature tools applied to urgent new problem\" not methodological innovation\n- **Deployment barriers** (R2-W5): Requires full response matrix access, sufficient model population, temporal labels\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n**Converged Score: 3.5/6 (Borderline — between Weak Reject and Weak Accept)**\n\n**Overall characterization:** The paper presents a conceptually valuable framework that bridges psychometric theory and LLM benchmark contamination — a genuine contribution. The experimental coverage is comprehensive (9 experiments covering detection, ensemble, equating, cross-benchmark, Bayesian accumulation, degradation analysis, and IFT). The calibrated narrative and transparent reporting of negative results demonstrate scientific maturity.\n\nHowever, three methodological issues prevent a confident accept: (1) the contamination-capability confound threatens the validity of all detection results, (2) the RL-robustness headline claim lacks adequate empirical support, and (3) the primary validation metric has circularity issues. The related work is also below venue standards.\n\n**Path to acceptance:** (1) Add within-family contamination-vs-capability analysis, (2) expand RL validation with equivalence testing and partial exploitation analysis, (3) reframe detection accuracy claims to acknowledge ground truth limitations, (4) expand related work to 35+ references including Tao et al. comparison, (5) cross-validate ensemble weights.\n\n**Recommendation:** Revise and resubmit. The core idea merits publication at a top venue, but the current execution leaves too many attack surfaces for confident acceptance at ~25% acceptance rate.","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260520T194223","overall_score":"borderline (3.5/6)","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":918,"cache_read_input_tokens":65240,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":918},"output_tokens":53,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"76f689bc-835b-4f00-b32c-50d6308bc7dd","uuid":"3cc10a07-7f06-4bec-b75d-19d7083c1a28"}
